$ $SPARK_HOME/bin/spark-submit groupbykey_median_home_prices.py
Spark version: 4.2.0

========================================================================
1. Sample data: (zip_code, price in $1000s)
========================================================================
partition 0 : [('95050', 1250), ('95014', 2900), ('95050', 9800), ('95112', 980), ('95050', 1400), ('95014', 3100)]
partition 1 : [('95050', 1310), ('95112', 1050), ('95014', 2750), ('95050', 1180), ('95112', 1120), ('95014', 3300)]
Every zip code has sales in BOTH partitions.

========================================================================
2. The groupByKey() solution
========================================================================
Step 1: all prices of each zip code
   95014 [2750, 2900, 3100, 3300]
   95050 [1180, 1250, 1310, 1400, 9800]
   95112 [980, 1050, 1120]

Step 2: (number of sales, median price)
   [('95014', (4, 3000.0)), ('95050', (5, 1310)), ('95112', (3, 1050))]

The report the website shows:
  95014 (Cupertino)   4 homes sold   median price: $3,000K
  95050 (Santa Clara)   5 homes sold   median price: $1,310K
  95112 (San Jose)   3 homes sold   median price: $1,050K

The (misleading) average, side by side with the median:
   95014 average: 3012.5   median: 3000.0
   95050 average: 2988.0   median: 1310
   95112 average: 1050.0   median: 1050
The one mansion in 95050 more than doubles the average.

========================================================================
3. Why partial medians cannot be merged
========================================================================
partial medians of 95050: [('95050', ([1250, 1400, 9800], 1400)), ('95050', ([1180, 1310], 1245.0))]
median of the partial medians: 1322.5  <- WRONG
the true median:               1310
The partial medians do not have enough information to recover the
true median, and the wrong answer depends on how Spark happened to
split the data. (The sum of partial sums, by contrast, is ALWAYS
the true sum: that's why reduceByKey() works for sums, not medians.)

========================================================================
4. Why not reduceByKey(), aggregateByKey(), or combineByKey()?
========================================================================
combineByKey(start_list, add_price, merge_lists):
   [('95014', (4, 3000.0)), ('95050', (5, 1310)), ('95112', (3, 1050))]
same answer? True

Same answer, but we just rewrote groupByKey() by hand: PySpark's own
groupByKey() is built from these very same three functions. When the
job really needs every value, say so: use groupByKey().

The price: EVERY value crosses the network, and all values of one key
must fit in one executor's memory. Fine here (a zip code has only a
few thousand sales a year); NOT fine for keys with billions of values.
